       privacy posture below. Tinted band; copy-left / before-after
       illustration-right on lg, stacked on mobile. The privacy points
       are detail rows in the left dl, because the privacy framing is
       the point as much as the accuracy win. -->
  <section id="voice-enrollment" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-[color:var(--color-border-subtle)] bg-[color:var(--color-section-tint)]">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-5xl items-start gap-10 px-6 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-14">
      <div>
      <p class="text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[color:var(--color-accent)]">
        Voice enrollment
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Teach it a word in your own voice.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        Some of the words you most want right are the ones that sound like something else. A product name like <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Keavi</strong> collides with the fruit <em>kiwi</em>; <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Claude</strong> collides with <em>clawed</em>. Spelling alone can't separate them — they sound the same. So Parleq lets you enroll a term by voice: read a few short sentences aloud, and it learns the sound of <em>you</em> saying that word. On later dictations it disambiguates by sound — recovering the name you meant, and leaving the everyday word untouched when that's the one you actually said.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
        It works alongside on-device cleanup, not instead of it — a sharper ear at the recognition stage, so the right word is already there before cleanup ever runs. And like everything in the recognition path, it stays on your Mac.
      </p>

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-6 border-l border-[color:var(--color-border)] pl-5">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A short read-aloud, once</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            The enrollment wizard shows a handful of carrier sentences that place the term in varied, natural contexts — beginning, middle, and end of a sentence — so it captures how the word actually sounds in speech rather than said in isolation. Speak each one; that's it. You can edit or regenerate any sentence first.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">A voiceprint, not a recording</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            What's kept is a mathematical <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">voiceprint</strong> — a compact embedding derived from the audio — never the audio itself. The recording is processed in memory and discarded; no enrollment audio is ever written to disk.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">On-device, no LLM, no cloud</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Both enrollment and the live, by-sound comparison run entirely on your Mac. No network call, no cloud service, and no LLM is involved in the disambiguation — it's an acoustic match against your enrolled voiceprint.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Encrypted at rest, device-only</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Voiceprints are biometric data, and Parleq treats them as such. They're stored as an <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">AES-GCM-encrypted</strong> blob under a key held in your Mac's Keychain that is <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-text)]">device-only and never synced</strong> — it isn't routed through iCloud Keychain, so the voiceprint can't leave the machine it was made on.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-text)]">Opt-in, per term, deletable anytime</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Enrollment is never automatic. You opt in once, and only for the specific terms you choose. Delete a single term's voiceprint, or wipe them all, whenever you like — and the encrypted file goes with it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      </div>

